      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-05-25T16:49:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Okay, measuring by pitting my mancomb versus my sasukette, I see a window of 4.3-4.5 ms in difference if I grab our timestamp at the previous disable location and print the elapsed time at our current disable location.  \n\nDoes this seem like an acceptable timing change?  The partner from the bug had about 16ms between the Accept and PS_RDY, so it seems like we should be covered in that case.  I can\u0027t find a minimum spec-required timing the partner needs to have from Accept to turning Vconn on to sending PS_RDY, but I would guess we\u0027re pretty well covered.",
      "parentUuid": "cc80aa23_0b270a74",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6311,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 6326,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "a9b35e7e8590a0897c4f9a995f7447bf26c671b1",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7e3f8d55_7c7e7ab2",
        "filename": "common/usbc/usb_pe_drp_sm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 6353,
      "author": {
        "id": 1305927
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-05-25T17:21:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think we can usefully send SOP\u0027 or SOP\u0027\u0027 Soft Reset here:\n* The cable shall not initiate traffic.\n* Even if it did, we shouldn\u0027t be able to receive SOP\u0027 or SOP\u0027\u0027 traffic right now.\n* Even if we received it, can we count on being able to send it after disabling SOP\u0027 reception? Does that depend on the TCPC?\n* Even if we send it, we shouldn\u0027t be able to receive the Accept, so we should expect to go into Hard Reset.\n* If, despite the above, we receive SOP\u0027 traffic, it\u0027s probably the port partner jumping the gun on enabling SOP\u0027. If we do SOP\u0027 Soft Reset in that situation, how will the port partner be expected to react?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6347,
        "startChar": 3,
        "endLine": 6353,
        "endChar": 45
      },
      "revId": "a9b35e7e8590a0897c4f9a995f7447bf26c671b1",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e30a7d60_38d9a374",
        "filename": "common/usbc/usb_pe_drp_sm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 6353,
      "author": {
        "id": 1287197
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-05-25T17:36:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, we shouldn\u0027t be receiving SOP\u0027 messages here unless we hit a narrow timing window of receiving something before this state\u0027s entry runs.  I think my general reasoning for using the SOP* of the incoming message is mostly that it\u0027s the behavior we should be doing from most states on non-interruptible AMS interruption and there\u0027s every chance this statement could be copy/pasted elsewhere in the future. \n\nWe are enabling SOP\u0027 on our way out (and exit runs before entry), so a soft reset on SOP\u0027 should go alright, in the sense that we can send the reset and receive the Accept and carry on from there.  \n\nI\u0027m not sure what will happen if it\u0027s actually the partner sending SOP\u0027 because that means there would potentially be three of us in the SOP\u0027 conversation at once.  There\u0027s a Cable Plug field in the message header that indicates whether the partner or cable plug sent the message, but there are no specific guidelines given for what to do on a mismatch (unlike data role which requires Type-C error recovery on mismatch, and power role which explicitly says to not use it for verification).",
      "parentUuid": "7e3f8d55_7c7e7ab2",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6347,
        "startChar": 3,
        "endLine": 6353,
        "endChar": 45
